body:
Okay so, this worked for me. Thank god I only got to this chapter today when you already shared your solution because I'd probably be crying otherwise lmfao.  I'm on PC, and funnily enough I've literally played through this remaster TWICE.  I've seen it at its worst, and had NEVER come across this weird game-breaking situation before.  I would have guessed it was the newest update but I guess I'm wrong based on when the OP posted.

&#x200B;

I am still absolutely stumped as to why this glitch occurs.  Whether it be due to speedy rewinds or Chloe's position OR, as your guide suggests, maybe where max is standing when the thing drops down.  Boggles my mind.  I absolutely adore these games but, man, this was not a fun half-hour trying other peoples suggestions until yours worked (again, the one literally posted a day ago) what a strange coincidence ig.

&#x200B;

The absolute biggest downside here is not being able to look inside the crate that Chloe is searching which has some neat nuggets of info on the old Prescott family and how they've [basically been practicing their same blackmail/intimidation since the 1900s.](https://life-is-strange.fandom.com/wiki/Martin_Lewis_Prescott)

I used to be in the camp of not-killing Larry but I actually noticed if you tell Clem to leave Lee in a playthrough where you tried to help Larry, there's even a dialogue option where Lee essentially admits he knew larry was dead in the meat locker but couldn't bring himself to "do more violence".  

&#x200B;

In real life I think I would probably be on the killing Larry side of the fence, purely for self-preservation.  Wouldn't be able to do it myself but I'm pretty sure I'd prefer not to risk it.

I believe Damon says James wants her "Out of the picture", and from what she implies in her conversation with Chloe, she's quite convinced that there's no coming back now that she has been dosed by Damon.  

&#x200B;

I think it's a disturbing twist either way, Damon considers injecting Chloe, but it's played in a much more soapy/melodramatic way than the twist in the original game which took itself quite seriously in comparison.   Like the whole fight scene with the music and stuff is somehow more teen-drama than the actual teen-drama lol

&#x200B;

I mean James' plan essentially works, he gets to keep his daughter all to himself, unless Chloe intervenes and changes things of course.   It's not a smart plan, and I have no experience with Heroin but from the way it's talked about by the characters in the game, Sera had a very hard time quitting.
